**Overloading** is having several functions with the **same name but different parameter lists** (number, types or order of parameters) in the same scope. It is resolved at **compile time**, so it is called static or compile-time polymorphism. Return type alone cannot distinguish overloads.

    class Calc {
        int add(int a, int b)        { return a + b; }
        double add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }
        int add(int a, int b, int c) { return a + b + c; }
    }

**Overriding** is when a **subclass redefines a method that the parent class already defines**, with the same name and parameters (and a compatible return type). It is resolved at **run time** based on the actual object type, which is run-time (dynamic) polymorphism.

    class Animal { void sound() { System.out.println("..."); } }
    class Dog extends Animal {
        @Override
        void sound() { System.out.println("Woof"); }
    }
    Animal a = new Dog();
    a.sound();   // prints Woof

**Key differences:**
- Scope: overloading within one class (or across inheritance); overriding needs inheritance.
- Signature: overloading changes it; overriding keeps it identical.
- Binding: compile time vs run time.
- Access: an overriding method cannot reduce visibility. In Java, `static`, `final` and `private` methods are not overridden (static methods are hidden).
- In C++, overriding needs a `virtual` function in the base class; without it you get hiding, not polymorphism. Base pointers to derived objects call the derived version through the vtable.

Mention `@Override` (Java) or `override` (C++11) as a safeguard against accidental mismatches, and note that overload resolution can be ambiguous with implicit conversions.
